le waiting for me they don't  know what I do they never will they're  protected but I do what I do so they can  have a better life and if I live or if I  die it really doesn't make a difference  to me as long as they have what they  need so when it's my time to go I will  go knowing I did everything I could for  them it's a powerful scene with great  delivery by Jonathan banks and given  what we know about how things turn out  for Mike trying to get his money to  Kaylee it really hits home as moving as  it is it's not the thing that gets Jimmy  up off the ground that turns out to be  the return of the truck of the guy who  got away Jimmy gets up wraps a space  blanket around him so that there's no  way you can miss him out there in the  Sun and he heads towards the road for a  second it felt like he was giving up and  that Mike would have to save him for a  second time but  as he's going he tells Mike to get ready  his plan is to draw the driver to him so  that Mike can take him out he'll play  decoy and if things go right they'll be  in an air-conditioned Bronco sipping  ice-cold water on the way into town this  makes for an intense scene where Mike  finally hits the driver with the second  shot which happens to be in the nick of  time since Jimmy appears to be too  exhausted to get out of the way if the  shot would have miss turns out there  won't be any aircon since the crash is  such that there isn't a chance they'll  be driving away right down to tires  being left behind on the road as Mike  was approaching the vehicle I was  thinking well at least I'll probably get  some water out of the deal and it's like  the writers could hear what I was  thinking when he pulls out the crust and  empty water jug we cut the Jimmy sitting  with his head in his hands a man with  very little left in his reserves he  finally takes the Davis and main bottle  that's filled with his pee it's been  tied around his shoulder for the entire  episode practically at this point and he  takes a long pull when urine urine and  with that he gets up to continue walking  as Saul Goodman an official friend of  the cartel the final shot is of a space  blanket laying on the road we see Mike  step on it as they leave it behind and  the episode ends speaking of the space  blanket I skipped over the other big  conversation in the desert where the  blanket was introduced I wanted to save  that to the end because it ties in with  the Kim storyline which is the real  tragedy of this episode Jimmy let it  slip that Kim would be worried when he  doesn't return home because she knows  why he came out to the desert in the  first place this sets off the alarm  bells for Mike who doesn't know Kim and  thinks she might tell someone about the  situation as viewers we know Kim is  smarter than that and really doesn't  have anyone to tell if you think about  it no family no close friends no  co-workers she confides in and enough  sense either way not to contact the  police  unfortunately this turns out to leave  her few options on what to do when Jimmy  doesn't come home and that leads her to  deciding to go see the client that sent  him to pick up the money no Kim no I  don't  this I don't want you to do it she did  it though and Lalo now knows she exists  he also knows she's no dummy after he  gets the opportunity to size her up in  person and he knows she's a lawyer which  he could surely use to his advantage it  is the worst case scenario and the only  bright spot is how good Tony Dalton is  at playing this character in Lalo  Salamanca as he's internalizing all the  ways things shifted in his favor like  him coming to see him he essentially  brushes her concerns aside and refuses  to help her by telling her where he sent  Jimmy to make the pickup he says it's  pointless because if he's alive he'll  show kim follows with and if he's but  can't say the words out loud it's a  crushing thought for her and she  realizes that taking this risk doesn't  pay off at all for her 